  "body": " Module 1: Assessment  Submitted Proofs and Reflection — Estimated time: 40 minutes    This assessment has three parts. Parts 1 and 2 ask you to write complete mathematical proofs, submitted for feedback. Part 3 is a short reflection. Submit all three parts together as a single document (PDF or typed file) through the course management system.   Write-up standard. All proofs must be written in complete mathematical prose. Use the style modeled in the Worked Examples: label your proof strategy in the opening sentence, write in complete grammatical sentences, and justify every step. Work that consists only of symbolic manipulations without explanation will not receive full credit.   A note on Part 2. All students complete all three parts. For Part 2, choose the option that best matches your background: if you are coming in with prior real analysis experience, challenge yourself with Option B. If analysis-style proofs are newer territory for you, Option A is the right starting point — work through it carefully before considering Option B.   Academic integrity. Your proofs must be your own work. You may refer to your notes, the textbooks, and the worked examples from this module. You may not collaborate with other students on the written proofs or use AI writing tools to draft your arguments.   Part 1: Core Proof (Required) (Estimated time: 20 minutes)  Recall the definition of sequence convergence:     Negating the Convergence Definition   Write, in careful symbolic notation, the statement that (the sequence does not converge to ). Your answer should be the formal negation of the definition above, with all quantifiers written explicitly and in the correct order.     Proving Convergence:   Let . Prove that .  Your proof must follow this structure:   Scratch work (clearly labeled, before the proof). Compute and find, given , an explicit (in terms of ) such that guarantees . You may use any algebraic manipulation here; this is your planning stage.   Formal proof. Using the you found in the scratch work, write a complete - proof in standard mathematical prose. The proof should begin: Let be given. Choose       Note that To make , you need . The Archimedean property (Corollary 2.4, Bauldry p. 48) guarantees that such an exists.     Applying the Negation   Using your negation from the first problem, write out what it would mean for the sequence to not converge to 3. (You do not need to prove this statement; just write it in symbols and translate it into a clear English sentence.)       Part 2: Proof Choice (Required — Choose One) (Estimated time: 15 minutes)  Choose one of the following two proof problems. Both problems ask you to write a complete proof; they differ in difficulty and which proof strategy you will use. Label your submission clearly with Option A or Option B.     Option A (Standard): A Divisibility Proof by Induction   Prove by mathematical induction that for all integers , (Here means 3 divides evenly, i.e., for some integer .)  Your proof must clearly label the base case, state the induction hypothesis, and complete the inductive step with full justification.    In the inductive step, write out , expand, and look for a way to use both the induction hypothesis and the fact that divides any multiple of .     Option B (More Challenging): Irrationality and a Contrapositive      Prove by contrapositive: for any integer , if is divisible by 5, then is divisible by 5.  (Hint: if , then leaves remainder 1, 2, 3, or 4 when divided by 5. Check each case to show . You may use proof by cases within the contrapositive argument.)    Using part (a), prove by contradiction that is irrational. Model your argument closely on the proof that is irrational; the key lemma you need is the result from part (a).          Part 3: Reflection (Estimated time: 5 minutes)  The following questions are short-answer and are not graded for mathematical correctness — they are graded for thoughtfulness and specificity. Write 3–5 sentences for each. These reflections help the instructor understand where you are and adapt the course accordingly.    Proof Process Reflection   Describe the process you used to write your proof in Part 1(b). Where did you get stuck, if at all? How did you decide on the value of ? What was the relationship between your scratch work and the final proof?     Bridge to Bauldry   In the Bridge Reading (Part 2), you examined Bauldry's proof of Theorem 2.3 (Archimedean Order Property). The proof used contradiction and the Least Upper Bound Property. Briefly explain the logical structure of that proof in your own words: what was assumed, what was constructed, and what contradiction was reached. (You are not writing a formal proof here; you are narrating the argument in plain English.)     Looking Ahead   The - proof you wrote in Part 1(b) is the first in a series: Module 2 will ask you to prove that various sequences converge using this same definition. Based on your experience with this proof, what do you anticipate will be the hardest step in applying the definition to more complex sequences? What would you want to practice more?      Assessment Rubric (Informational)   The following rubric describes how your submitted proofs will be evaluated. Reading it before you write will help you focus your efforts.    Grading Criteria        Criterion  Full credit  Partial credit  Minimal credit    Logical correctness  All steps are logically valid; the argument proves exactly the stated claim  Argument is mostly correct with one fixable gap  Argument has significant logical errors or proves the wrong statement    Quantifier handling  Quantifiers are written correctly, in the right order, with the right universe  Minor error in notation but order is correct  Quantifier order is wrong or domains are missing    Proof strategy  Strategy is named; proof follows the announced structure throughout  Strategy is correct but not announced, or announced but not fully followed  Strategy is inappropriate for the claim    Mathematical prose  Complete grammatical sentences; no dangling symbols; clear flow  Mostly prose with some notational shorthand where prose is needed  Primarily symbolic calculation without connecting sentences    Reflection  Specific, thoughtful, and shows engagement with the mathematical content  General but relevant observations  Vague or too brief to be informative     "
